      <h1 id="accessing-the-registry" class="group scroll-mt-20">Accessing the registry<a href="#accessing-the-registry" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p class="_abstract">You can access a registry to view logs and metrics. You can also secure and expose the registry.</p>
      <p>After you logged in to the registry by using the <code>podman login</code> command, you can push or pull images from the integrated registry directly by using <code>podman push</code> or <code>podman pull</code> commands. The commands that you can use depend on your user permissions.</p>
      <h2 id="_prerequisites" class="group scroll-mt-20">Prerequisites<a href="#_prerequisites" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You must have configured an identity provider (IDP).</li>
      <li>
      For pulling images, for example when using the <code>podman pull</code> command, the user must have the <code>registry-viewer</code> role. To add this role, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc policy add-role-to-user registry-viewer <user_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      For writing or pushing images, such as using <code>podman push</code> command, complete the following steps:
      <ul>
      <li>
      Your account has the <code>registry-editor</code> role. To add this role, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc policy add-role-to-user registry-editor <user_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Your cluster must have an existing project where the images can be pushed to.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="registry-accessing-directly_accessing-the-registry" class="group scroll-mt-20">Accessing the registry directly from the cluster<a href="#registry-accessing-directly_accessing-the-registry" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can access the registry from inside the cluster by using internal routes.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Access the node by getting its name:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc debug nodes/<node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To enable access to tools such as <code>oc</code> and <code>podman</code> on the node, change your root directory to <code>/host</code>. Successful output on running the commands states <code>Login Succeeded!</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# chroot /host"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Log in to the container image registry by using your access token:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# oc login -u kubeadmin -p <password_from_install_log> https://api-int.<cluster_name>.<base_domain>:6443"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# podman login -u kubeadmin -p $(oc whoami -t) image-registry.openshift-image-registry.svc:5000"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You can pass almost any value for the user name. The token contains all necessary information. Passing a user name that contains colons results in a login failure.</p>
          <p>The Image Registry Operator creates the route, such as <code>default-route-openshift-image-registry.&lt;cluster_name&gt;</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Perform <code>podman pull</code> and <code>podman push</code> operations against your registry. The following example commands demonstrate these operations.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Pull an arbitrary image:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# podman pull <name.io>/<image>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You can pull arbitrary images, but if you have the <strong>system:registry</strong> role added, you can only push images to the registry in your project.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Tag the new image with the form <code>&lt;registry_ip&gt;:&lt;port&gt;/&lt;project&gt;/&lt;image&gt;</code>. For example, <code>172.30.124.220:5000/openshift/image</code>. The project name must show in the pull specification for OpenShift Container Platform to correctly place and later access the image in the registry.
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# podman tag <name.io>/<image> image-registry.openshift-image-registry.svc:5000/openshift/<image>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You must have the <code>system:image-builder</code> role for the specified project, which allows the user to write or push an image. Otherwise, the <code>podman push</code> in the next step will fail. To test, you can create a new project to push the image.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Push the newly tagged image to your registry:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# podman push image-registry.openshift-image-registry.svc:5000/openshift/<image>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>When pushing images to the internal registry, the repository name must use the <code>&lt;project&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code> format. Using multiple project levels in the repository name results in an authentication error.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="checking-the-status-of-registry-pods_accessing-the-registry" class="group scroll-mt-20">Checking the status of the registry pods<a href="#checking-the-status-of-registry-pods_accessing-the-registry" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>As a cluster administrator,</p>
      <p class="_abstract">you can list the image registry pods running in the <code>openshift-image-registry</code> project and check their status.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li></ul>
